# UI code structure and responsibilities

## UI Code rules

- event handlers must be created before rendering. Do not add it inline in DOM
- className must be resolved before added to DOM
- split component into single responsibility.
- more smaller components are better than one big component
- any text in component use Text component
- any complex className must be resolved using function in ui-utils
- Use scss files

## File split (required)

- Keep UI code split into distinct files:
  - `types.ts`: types for the component/hook (props, state shape, options, return types)
  - `<Component>.tsx`: rendering only
  - `<Component>.module.scss`: all styles (scss modules)
  - `<Component>.stories.tsx`: Storybook stories for all supported variants/states
  - `<Component>.test.tsx`: tests colocated with the component when applicable

## Constants (required)

- If any constants are added to component:
  - Create a `constants.ts` file and move it all there.
- For constants consider: static text, numbers, map objects

## Component vs hook responsibilities (required)

- `<Component>.tsx` must be focused on rendering:
  - no business logic, no derived state orchestration, no side-effects
  - keep event handlers as thin delegations to hook actions
- If the component has logic, create a dedicated hook and use it from the component:
  - `use<Component>.ts` holds state, derived values, effects, memoization, callbacks
  - the hook returns a small “view model” (values + handlers) consumed by the component

## Composition and subcomponents (required)

- Component is considered as subcomponent if it's:
  - list
  - item in list
  - dom tree deeper than 3 layers
- If a component is composed of multiple meaningful parts, create subcomponents:
  - extract each part into its own `*.tsx` (and `*.module.scss` if needed)
  - subcomponents should also be render-focused; shared logic stays in hooks

## Next.js web app (`apps/web`, App Router)

### Composition-first (required)

- The web app **composes existing UI** from `ui/*` (and other shared packages): import components, pass props, and arrange them on the page or in layouts. **Do not add new reusable UI** (primitives, widgets, feature blocks, hooks-for-UI) under `apps/web`—implement those in a `ui/*` package and consume them here.
- **Narrow exception — page shells only**: you may add a component under `apps/web` **only** when it exists solely to keep `page.tsx` / `layout.tsx` small by **grouping** already-built pieces into a recognizable region (examples: page header band, footer block, sidebar column, main + aside grid). Shells must be thin: mostly composition of `ui/*` children, optional wrapper markup, and light layout styling. No business rules, no new visual language, no hooks beyond trivial layout (e.g. open/close sidebar)—if it needs real logic or becomes reusable, move it to `ui/*`.
- Do not use `apps/web` as a second component library: no Storybook targets here for shells unless you explicitly need them; real components follow the full file split and stories in `ui/*`.

### Top-level layout

- `app/`: route segments only (`page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `template.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`, `route.ts`, `default.tsx` where needed). Keep these files thin: metadata, segment wiring, and composition—no large component trees or business logic.
- `app/globals.scss` (or `globals.css` if global tokens only): app-wide base styles; avoid piling feature styles here.
- `lib/`: pure helpers, server/client-safe utilities, and small adapters (no JSX). Split by concern (`lib/auth/`, `lib/api/`, etc.) when a file would exceed the general line-length guideline.
- **Shell components (exception only)**: colocate next to the route (e.g. `app/(dashboard)/_components/PageShell.tsx`) or under a small `apps/web/components/shells/` (or similar) if shared across a few routes. Names should reflect structure (`DashboardSidebar`, `MarketingFooter`), not feature implementation.

### What lives where

- **Route file (`page.tsx`, etc.)**: fetch or delegate data loading (Server Components), pass props into components from `ui/*`, set `metadata` / `generateMetadata` in the same segment or a nearby `metadata.ts` pattern—keep orchestration readable; move heavy logic to `lib/` or server-only modules.
- **Interactive UI**: the interactive piece should live in `ui/*` with `'use client'` on the smallest export that needs it; the route stays a Server Component when possible. Do not create ad-hoc client components in `apps/web` except allowed shells that only wrap `ui/*` clients.
- **API / webhooks**: implement in `route.ts` next to the segment or under a dedicated `app/api/.../route.ts`; keep handlers thin and delegate to `lib/` or domain packages.
- **Middleware**: `middleware.ts` at `apps/web` root for cross-cutting request logic only; keep it short and test helpers in `lib/`.

### File split inside the app

- **Packages (`ui/*`)**: follow the full split in this document (`types.ts`, `<Component>.tsx`, `*.module.scss`, `use*.ts`, stories, tests).
- **Allowed shells in `apps/web`**: keep minimal—often a single `<Shell>.tsx` plus `<Shell>.module.scss` if layout needs scoped styles; avoid extra files unless the shell would exceed the usual line-length limit, then split only for readability (still no new design primitives).
- Do not define multiple unrelated pieces in `page.tsx` or `layout.tsx`: either import from `ui/*` or extract **only** an allowed shell that groups those imports.

### Server vs client modules

- Default is Server Components; only mark files with `'use client'` when required. Prefer client boundaries on `ui/*` modules; in `apps/web`, `'use client'` on a shell is only for trivial layout state that cannot live in `ui/*`—otherwise extend `ui/*`.
- Do not import server-only modules (secrets, direct DB, filesystem) into client components or shared modules that client code imports. Isolate server-only code in files that never ship to the client boundary (e.g. used only from `page.tsx` / `layout.tsx` / `route.ts` without re-export to client).

## Exports

- Use named exports (no default exports), consistent with repo conventions.
- **Exception — Next.js route modules**: `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `template.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`, and `default.tsx` must use the `default` export required by the App Router. Implement real views as **named** exports in `ui/*` (with tests/stories there); route files default-export a thin composition. Allowed shells in `apps/web` use **named** exports.
